{
  "Setup guide": [
    {
      "row": 2,
      "cells": [
        {
          "cell": "B2",
          "value": "Upgrading Hadeer's returns sheet - in place",
          "formula": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "row": 3,
      "cells": [
        {
          "cell": "B3",
          "value": "Google Sheet \"return manager\"  |  about 15 minutes, once  |  nothing she does today breaks",
          "formula": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "row": 5,
      "cells": [
        {
          "cell": "B5",
          "value": "WHAT YOU ARE ADDING",
          "formula": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "row": 6,
      "cells": [
        {
          "cell": "C6",
          "value": "Six new columns, J to O",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "D6",
          "value": "Her existing columns A to I are untouched. Column F, the free-text return reason, stays exactly as it is - it is the most valuable field in the whole system and nothing replaces it.",
          "formula": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "row": 7,
      "cells": [
        {
          "cell": "C7",
          "value": "Two optional, P and Q",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "D7",
          "value": "Refund EGP and Recoverable. Add them only if you want refund totals in the workbook. Everything else works without them.",
          "formula": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "row": 9,
      "cells": [
        {
          "cell": "B9",
          "value": "STEP BY STEP",
          "formula": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "row": 10,
      "cells": [
        {
          "cell": "B10",
          "value": "1",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "C10",
          "value": "Add a Lists tab",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "D10",
          "value": "In her sheet: bottom bar, plus icon, rename the new tab to Lists. Then open the 'Paste into Lists' tab of this file, copy the whole block A1:L26, and paste it into Lists!A1. This tab holds every dropdown value plus the two translation tables the migration formulas need.",
          "formula": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "row": 11,
      "cells": [
        {
          "cell": "B11",
          "value": "2",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "C11",
          "value": "Protect the Lists tab",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "D11",
          "value": "Right-click the Lists tab, Protect sheet, restrict to yourself. If this tab gets edited the dropdowns drift and the workbook stops recognising her answers.",
          "formula": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "row": 12,
      "cells": [
        {
          "cell": "B12",
          "value": "3",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "C12",
          "value": "Type the six new headers",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "D12",
          "value": "In Sheet1, go to J1 and type across: return type, call status, call attempts, root cause, size detail, resolution. Exact spelling, lowercase. The workbook reads by position, but you will thank yourself later.",
          "formula": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "row": 13,
      "cells": [
        {
          "cell": "B13",
          "value": "4",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "C13",
          "value": "Add the dropdowns",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "D13",
          "value": "Select J2:J1000, Data > Data validation > Dropdown (from a range) > Lists!E2:E4. Repeat for the other four: K uses Lists!B2:B8, M uses Lists!A2:A26, N uses Lists!C2:C9, O uses Lists!D2:D8. Leave L (call attempts) as a plain number column.",
          "formula": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "row": 14,
      "cells": [
        {
          "cell": "B14",
          "value": "5",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "C14",
          "value": "Set rejection on invalid input",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "D14",
          "value": "In each of those validation rules, choose 'Reject the input' rather than 'Show a warning'. A warning gets clicked through; a rejection is what actually keeps the taxonomy clean.",
          "formula": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "row": 15,
      "cells": [
        {
          "cell": "B15",
          "value": "6",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "C15",
          "value": "Run the migration once",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "D15",
          "value": "Open the 'Migration formulas' tab of this file and paste each formula into row 2 of its column, then drag down to the last row. They read her old columns G and H and fill the new ones automatically - no risk of anything landing on the wrong row.",
          "formula": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "row": 16,
      "cells": [
        {
          "cell": "B16",
          "value": "7",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "C16",
          "value": "Freeze the formulas",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "D16",
          "value": "Select J2:O1000, Ctrl+C, then Ctrl+Shift+V to paste as values only. This is important. If you leave them as formulas, the moment Hadeer types over one it breaks the column.",
          "formula": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "row": 17,
      "cells": [
        {
          "cell": "B17",
          "value": "8",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "C17",
          "value": "Add the duplicate check",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "D17",
          "value": "Put the formula from the 'Migration formulas' tab into S2 and drag down. It flags any order ID entered twice. You paste new returns weekly, so this is what stops the same return being worked and counted twice.",
          "formula": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "row": 18,
      "cells": [
        {
          "cell": "B18",
          "value": "9",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "C18",
          "value": "Colour the new block",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "D18",
          "value": "Select J1:O1 and give it a fill so she can see at a glance where her new work starts. Yellow works.",
          "formula": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "row": 19,
      "cells": [
        {
          "cell": "B19",
          "value": "10",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "C19",
          "value": "Walk her through it once",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "D19",
          "value": "Fifteen minutes with her, live, on three real returns. The SOP alone will not do it - the dropdown habit has to be built once by hand.",
          "formula": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "row": 21,
      "cells": [
        {
          "cell": "B21",
          "value": "THE ONE THING TO EXPLAIN TO HER",
          "formula": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "row": 22,
      "cells": [
        {
          "cell": "C22",
          "value": "Stop using 2/2",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "D22",
          "value": "She has been writing 2/2 in the Follow up column to mean two call attempts. That is genuinely useful information and it is why her data looked broken on export - Excel converts it to a date. From now on that goes in two places instead: call status = No Answer, call attempts = 2. Tell her the old convention was right, the column was wrong.",
          "formula": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "row": 23,
      "cells": [
        {
          "cell": "C23",
          "value": "Root cause only when reached",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "D23",
          "value": "If she did not speak to the customer, root cause stays blank. A guess there is worse than a gap, because a guess gets counted.",
          "formula": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "row": 24,
      "cells": [
        {
          "cell": "C24",
          "value": "Size detail is new",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "D24",
          "value": "When the reason is fit, say which way it was wrong. Too tight and too loose lead to opposite fixes and the old sheet could not tell them apart.",
          "formula": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "row": 26,
      "cells": [
        {
          "cell": "B26",
          "value": "WHAT NEEDS A HUMAN PASS",
          "formula": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "row": 27,
      "cells": [
        {
          "cell": "C27",
          "value": "Rows marked resolved",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "D27",
          "value": "About five rows have Follow up = resolved. That could mean an exchange, a reship or a refund - the old sheet did not record which. The migration leaves resolution blank on those. Go through them once and pick the right value.",
          "formula": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "row": 28,
      "cells": [
        {
          "cell": "C28",
          "value": "The customer problem entries",
          "formula": null
        },
        {
          "cell": "D28",
          "value": "A category called 'customer problem' appeared this month. Reading the note, it maps to Changed mind, owner Customer. The migration does that automatically. If you read it differently, change the mapping in Lists!I:J before running step 6.",
          "formula": null
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
